@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Make it production") {
        The task app from the previous chapters works. This chapter makes
        it trustworthy: destructive actions ask before they act, data loads
        asynchronously instead of appearing by magic, and you meet the
        debug tools that every TerminalUI debug build carries for free.

        Continue from the third tutorial's finished code, or
        [download the finished project](https://dardevelin.github.io/terminal-ui/downloads/build-a-task-list.zip)
        as the base to edit.
    }

    @Section(title: "Destructive actions ask first") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Deleting a task is the first thing in the app that cannot be
            undone, so it is the first thing that should ask. The
            `.confirmationDialog` modifier on ``TerminalView`` presents a
            stacked-action dialog while a Bool binding is true — Escape
            dismisses it, Enter presses the focused button, and any
            ``AlertAction`` closes it after running.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add a delete key. The dialog attaches to the root
                ``TabView`` so it presents over everything, and the
                presentation flag `confirmDelete` lives on the store next
                to the data it protects: the same flag the
                `.confirmationDialog` binding reads is the one `onEvent`
                checks to keep app bindings quiet while the dialog is up.

                Backspace, not a letter: handled keys still reach the
                focused ``PromptInput``, so a `d` chord would also type
                itself into the draft — the same reasoning that gated `q`.
                With an empty draft, Backspace has nothing to erase and is
                free to mean "delete task". Press it: the dialog appears,
                with focus on Cancel and Delete rendered in the theme's
                destructive color.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks6-01-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks6-01-01.png", alt: "A Delete task? dialog floating over the app, with stacked Cancel and Delete buttons.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Load, don't block") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Real apps do not get their data for free at launch — it comes
            from a file or a network, and the UI has to say so instead of
            freezing. The `.task` modifier on ``TerminalView`` owns async
            work for exactly as long as the view is on screen, and a
            ``Spinner`` shows that something is happening while the list
            is still empty.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Drop the seeded tasks and load them from a `.task` on the
                tasks screen. The sleeps stand in for the real read; each
                tick advances the spinner frame, and mutating the store
                wakes the render loop — nothing anywhere schedules a
                redraw. When the view leaves the screen, the framework
                cancels the task; the `id:` variant goes further and
                restarts the work whenever its identity value changes.

                Run it: a spinner and "Loading tasks…" hold the list's
                place, then the loaded tasks land and the app behaves
                exactly as before.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks6-02-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks6-02-01.png", alt: "The task list replaced by a spinner and a Loading tasks message while the load runs.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "The debug story") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Nothing in this app opts into debugging, yet every TerminalUI
            debug tool already works against it — the tools ship in the
            framework behind `#if DEBUG`, so a debug build has them all
            and a release build compiles every trace of them away.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Finish the polish: the header count becomes a status light
                (gray "loading", yellow "N open", green "all done"), and
                the detail pane says "Loading…" while the load runs.

                Then run the app and press **F1** — live frame stats float
                over your UI. **F2** opens the debug panel; **F3** drops an
                inspector you can move over any cell to see which view
                owns it. From another shell, `swift run swtui ctl
                screenshot` captures the app's cells over its per-process
                socket, and `swtui ctl tree` prints the live view graph —
                the same tools work on any TerminalUI app you will ever
                build, including this one, with zero setup.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks6-03-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks6-03-01.png", alt: "The finished app: a checked-off task rendered dim, a yellow open count, and the detail pane showing done in green.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            Why does `confirmDelete` live on the store instead of inside the dialog?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Both the presenting modifier and `onEvent` read the same
                flag — presentation is app state, so the key handling can
                stand aside exactly while the dialog is up.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    One source of truth drives the dialog's visibility and
                    the keyboard gating; there is no second registry of
                    "open dialogs" to keep in sync.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Dialogs cannot hold their own state.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    A view can own state — but then `onEvent`, which runs
                    before the view layer, could not see whether a dialog
                    is up.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Store properties render faster than view-local state.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Performance is identical — visibility to the app's
                    event handling is what the store placement buys.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            What does the `id:` parameter of `.task(id:)` change?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                It names the task so the debugger can find it.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    The id is not a label — it is an identity value the
                    framework compares between renders.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                The work is cancelled and restarted whenever the id's
                value changes; without an id it starts once and runs until
                the view disappears.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    That is the shape for "load the thing this view is
                    currently showing" — change the selection, and the old
                    load is cancelled before the new one starts.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                It makes the task run on a background thread.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    The closure is main-actor either way, so it can touch
                    your `@Observable` store directly; awaits inside still
                    suspend as usual.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            What did the task app have to do to make F3 and `swtui ctl` work against it?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Link a separate debug library and register the app with
                `swtui`.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    There is nothing to link or register — the tools live
                    inside TerminalUI behind `#if DEBUG`.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Nothing, and the tools also ship in release builds.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Half right: nothing to do, but a release build
                    compiles all debug machinery away — zero overhead is
                    the point.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Nothing — any debug build has the F-key tools and the
                control socket; release builds compile them away.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    The inspector, overlay, panel, and the socket behind
                    `swtui ctl screenshot` / `tree` come with every debug
                    build of every TerminalUI app.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
